{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Bj6mwh5VHkWo1kRDZ1qiy1","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Draft reference gap analysis\n2\t\n3\t**Status:** ready. There are 3 targets, and only the first is high priority.\n4\t\n5\tThe PPI comparator family in Related Work is already well cited. It covers PPI, PPI++, cross-PPI, stratified PPI and about a dozen variants. The quantitative claims are grounded in `paper_data.json`, so none of them need citations. The gaps below are the ones where retrieval would materially help.\n6\t\n7\t## Missing literatures\n8\t- Control variates and augmented / efficient estimation with missing outcomes (AIPW, semi-supervised mean estimation).\n9\t- Provenance of the PPI-style benchmark pools.\n10\t- Isotonic regression and recalibration for continuous outcomes.\n11\t\n12\t## Targets\n13\t\n14\t1. **control-variate-efficiency-positioning** (Related Work, high, positioning)\n15\t   - Anchor: \"The control-variate form of PPI has antecedents in semi-supervised mean estimation\".\n16\t   - The thesis rests on PPI++ being a control-variate estimator and on a headroom formula (R² = η² when E[Y|Ŷ] is in the feature set). That formula restates semiparametric efficiency results.\n17\t   - The paragraph has only two semi-supervised citations and one DML citation. Classical control variates and AIPW / missing-data efficiency theory are uncited, so reviewers will likely ask what is new.\n18\t   - Search topics: AIPW semiparametric efficiency for mean estimation with missing outcomes; optimal control-variate coefficient; relation of PPI to AIPW.\n19\t\n20\t2. **benchmark-pool-provenance** (Method, evaluation protocol, medium, benchmark_context)\n21\t   - Anchor: \"a plankton-imaging pool whose label and prediction are both binary\".\n22\t   - The plankton pool and the search pools (Amazon deforestation, gene expression, protein disorder) have no sources. The intro's remote-sensing and galaxy-morphology motivation is also uncited. Only Galaxy Zoo and the census pools are cited.\n23\t   - Search topics: plankton imaging dataset; Amazon deforestation dataset; gene expression sequence-to-expression dataset; protein disorder with AlphaFold.\n24\t\n25\t3. **isotonic-recalibration-regression** (Related Work, Calibration, low, background)\n26\t   - Anchor: \"Isotonic and binned maps from scores to outcome probabilities are standard calibration tools\".\n27\t   - The cited calibration papers address probabilities. The method and the η² estimate use isotonic regression for continuous outcomes.\n28\t   - Search topics: isotonic regression for conditional mean estimation; recalibration of regression predictions.\n29\t\n30\t## Not pursued\n31\t- Post-selection / adaptive interval selection. The draft states plainly that the 1% fallback rule has no guarantee, so this would be speculative expansion.\n32\t- More PPI variants. Several bib entries are not cited in the text (credit risk, cardiac, etc.) and are off-topic. The strategy file says not to cite them.\n33\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"00c62331-6e73-4de3-bf79-36be3462f76f","uuid":"f0cbb27f-7455-4550-94c5-a6d3428f4e19","timestamp":"2026-11-11T12:03:28.054Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/paper/draft_reference_gaps.md","content":"# Draft reference gap analysis\n\n**Status:** ready. There are 3 targets, and only the first is high priority.\n\nThe PPI comparator family in Related Work is already well cited. It covers PPI, PPI++, cross-PPI, stratified PPI and about a dozen variants. The quantitative claims are grounded in `paper_data.json`, so none of them need citations. The gaps below are the ones where retrieval would materially help.\n\n## Missing literatures\n- Control variates and augmented / efficient estimation with missing outcomes (AIPW, semi-supervised mean estimation).\n- Provenance of the PPI-style benchmark pools.\n- Isotonic regression and recalibration for continuous outcomes.\n\n## Targets\n\n1. **control-variate-efficiency-positioning** (Related Work, high, positioning)\n   - Anchor: \"The control-variate form of PPI has antecedents in semi-supervised mean estimation\".\n   - The thesis rests on PPI++ being a control-variate estimator and on a headroom formula (R² = η² when E[Y|Ŷ] is in the feature set). That formula restates semiparametric efficiency results.\n   - The paragraph has only two semi-supervised citations and one DML citation. Classical control variates and AIPW / missing-data efficiency theory are uncited, so reviewers will likely ask what is new.\n   - Search topics: AIPW semiparametric efficiency for mean estimation with missing outcomes; optimal control-variate coefficient; relation of PPI to AIPW.\n\n2. **benchmark-pool-provenance** (Method, evaluation protocol, medium, benchmark_context)\n   - Anchor: \"a plankton-imaging pool whose label and prediction are both binary\".\n   - The plankton pool and the search pools (Amazon deforestation, gene expression, protein disorder) have no sources. The intro's remote-sensing and galaxy-morphology motivation is also uncited. Only Galaxy Zoo and the census pools are cited.\n   - Search topics: plankton imaging dataset; Amazon deforestation dataset; gene expression sequence-to-expression dataset; protein disorder with AlphaFold.\n\n3. **isotonic-recalibration-regression** (Related Work, Calibration, low, background)\n   - Anchor: \"Isotonic and binned maps from scores to outcome probabilities are standard calibration tools\".\n   - The cited calibration papers address probabilities. The method and the η² estimate use isotonic regression for continuous outcomes.\n   - Search topics: isotonic regression for conditional mean estimation; recalibration of regression predictions.\n\n## Not pursued\n- Post-selection / adaptive interval selection. The draft states plainly that the 1% fallback rule has no guarantee, so this would be speculative expansion.\n- More PPI variants. Several bib entries are not cited in the text (credit risk, cardiac, etc.) and are off-topic. The strategy file says not to cite them.\n","numLines":33,"startLine":1,"totalLines":33}}}
