{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Jskgr4WZLarg7phTS39fZf","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t{\n2\t  \"status\": \"ready\",\n3\t  \"summary\": \"The PPI comparator family is densely cited, but the control-variate/augmented-estimator framing, the sources of the benchmark pools, and the isotonic-regression recalibration step lack direct citations.\",\n4\t  \"missing_literatures\": [\n5\t    \"control variates and augmented / efficient estimation with missing outcomes (AIPW, semi-supervised mean estimation)\",\n6\t    \"provenance of the PPI-style benchmark pools (plankton imaging, Amazon deforestation, gene expression, protein disorder / AlphaFold)\",\n7\t    \"isotonic regression and recalibration for continuous outcomes\"\n8\t  ],\n9\t  \"rewrite_targets\": [\n10\t    {\n11\t      \"target_id\": \"control-variate-efficiency-positioning\",\n12\t      \"section\": \"related work\",\n13\t      \"priority\": \"high\",\n14\t      \"citation_intent\": \"positioning\",\n15\t      \"reason\": \"The paper's central thesis is that PPI++ is a control-variate estimator and that headroom is set by the efficient influence function / conditional mean. Related Work covers this with only two semi-supervised citations and a DML citation. Classical control variates and augmented-IPW / missing-data efficiency theory, which the headroom result (R^2 = eta^2 with m(Yhat) in the feature set) restates, are uncited. A reviewer would likely ask how the result differs from known semiparametric efficiency results.\",\n16\t      \"anchor_text\": \"The control-variate form of PPI has antecedents in semi-supervised mean estimation\",\n17\t      \"search_topics\": [\n18\t        \"augmented inverse probability weighting semiparametric efficiency missing outcomes mean estimation\",\n19\t        \"control variates variance reduction optimal coefficient\",\n20\t        \"relation of prediction-powered inference to semiparametric efficient estimation / AIPW\"\n21\t      ]\n22\t    },\n23\t    {\n24\t      \"target_id\": \"benchmark-pool-provenance\",\n25\t      \"section\": \"method\",\n26\t      \"priority\": \"medium\",\n27\t      \"citation_intent\": \"benchmark_context\",\n28\t      \"reason\": \"The evaluation protocol names a plankton-imaging pool and search pools (Amazon deforestation, gene expression, protein disorder) with no citation. Only the Galaxy Zoo and census pools have sources. The intro's remote-sensing and galaxy-morphology motivation is also uncited. Sources of benchmark data are expected, and these pools are the ones used in the PPI benchmark literature.\",\n29\t      \"anchor_text\": \"a plankton-imaging pool whose label and prediction are both binary\",\n30\t      \"search_topics\": [\n31\t        \"plankton imaging classification dataset\",\n32\t        \"Amazon deforestation remote sensing forest cover dataset\",\n33\t        \"gene expression / promoter sequence-to-expression dataset\",\n34\t        \"protein intrinsic disorder prediction AlphaFold structure\"\n35\t      ]\n36\t    },\n37\t    {\n38\t      \"target_id\": \"isotonic-recalibration-regression\",\n39\t      \"section\": \"related work\",\n40\t      \"priority\": \"low\",\n41\t      \"citation_intent\": \"background\",\n42\t      \"reason\": \"The Calibration paragraph cites only probability-calibration papers (Zadrozny, Niculescu-Mizil). The method uses isotonic and binned maps to estimate E[Y|Yhat] for continuous outcomes, and the headroom analysis estimates eta^2 with an isotonic fit. Foundational isotonic-regression references would support these steps directly.\",\n43\t      \"anchor_text\": \"Isotonic and binned maps from scores to outcome probabilities are standard calibration tools\",\n44\t      \"search_topics\": [\n45\t        \"isotonic regression monotone conditional mean estimation\",\n46\t        \"recalibration of regression predictions\"\n47\t      ]\n48\t    }\n49\t  ],\n50\t  \"failure_reason\": \"\"\n51\t}\n52\t"}]},