s (galaxy morphology, Amazon deforestation, gene expression, protein disorder, plus synthetic regimes; 60 draws per family): the recalibrated interval above, a label-free spline-basis control variate with leave-one-out PRESS model selection, and a repeated-cross-fitted calibrator stack combined by non-negative least squares. These are single evaluations on search pools and are reported as a contrast in Section~\\ref{sec:search}, not as controlled ablations; the search pools are disjoint from the sealed pools.\n136:Most of the gain over labeled-only inference is therefore already captured by PPI machinery; recalibration adds a small increment on top. Per family, the narrowing against PPI++ is 0.0287 for census income, 0.0018 for census insurance, 0.0023 for census education, 0.0359 for plankton and 0.0413 for the synthetic mixture, corresponding to derived width reductions of 2.8\\%, 0.2\\%, 0.2\\%, 3.5\\% and 4.0\\%. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics]] @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#derived_width_reduction_vs_ppi_pp]]\n158:plankton & 0.0359 & 3.5\\% & 0.0000 & 0.0000 & 0.0359 \\\\\n168:We evaluated Eq.~\\eqref{eq:ceiling} on each pool, using $\\rho^2$ and an isotonic estimate of $\\eta^2$ on the full pool, $n=500$, $N=20000$, and the evaluator's PPI++ weight clip (for synthetic regimes, large-sample moments of the generator and the evaluator's episode plan). The population ceiling is not a bound on a single finite draw, and it excludes the estimation cost of $g$.\n174:\\emph{Where the observed gain exceeds the ceiling.} In four of five families the observed narrowing is larger than the ceiling (Table~\\ref{tab:final}). The plankton pool is the clearest case: both $Y$ and $\\Yhat$ are binary, $\\E[Y\\mid\\Yhat]$ is therefore exactly linear in $\\Yhat$, the ceiling is zero, yet the observed narrowing is 0.0359 (3.5\\%). % @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#real.plankton]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.log_ratio_vs_ppi_family_plankton]]\n202:Figure~\\ref{fig:search} compares the three model variants, each evaluated once on the search pools. Their aggregate scores against PPI++ are 0.0271 (recalibrated isotonic/binned), 0.0223 (stacked calibration) and 0.0046 (label-free basis with PRESS); all three were scored and passed the evaluator's validity gate. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#grounding_sources.result.top_program_01]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#grounding_sources.result.top_program_02]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#grounding_sources.result.top_program_03]]\n203:The recalibrated variant is largest on the synthetic family (0.0757) and gene expression (0.0290), and smallest on galaxy morphology (0.0035). % @evidence[[source_evidence/search_metrics/recalibrated_metrics.json]]\n204:The stacked variant tracks it on the synthetic family (0.0707) and gene expression (0.0278) but is smaller on deforestation and protein disorder; the smooth label-free basis is close to PPI++ on the synthetic family (0.0034), where the nonlinearity is the exponential regime of the search generator. % @evidence[[source_evidence/search_metrics/stacked_metrics.json]] @evidence[[source_evidence/search_metrics/label_free_basis_metrics.json]]\n218:\\paragraph{What it does not support.} The evidence does not show that the narrowing is free: coverage point estimates are 0.934 pooled and 0.91 for the lowest family, below 0.95, and the baselines' coverage is unknown. The observed gain exceeds the ceiling in four of five families, so a part of it is not explained by recalibration, and plankton in particular cannot be attributed to a nonlinear calibration. The paper does not claim superiority beyond the pools evaluated, validity under the selection rule beyond the empirical gate, or generalization to other estimands or to covariate shift.\n242:Settings of the evaluated interval: $K=5$ shuffled folds seeded from the candidate's random generator; isotonic fit with clipped out-of-bounds prediction; binned fit with $\\min(10,\\max(2,\\lfloor 0.8n/25\\rfloor))$ bins (10 for $n=500$, 6 for $n=200$); pooled standardization of features; ridge $10^{-6}\\,\\mathrm{tr}(A)/p$ on the weight system; Student-$t$ quantile via a Cornish--Fisher expansion with degrees of freedom floored at 3; PPI++ weight clipped to $[0,1]$; fallback margin 0.99. The reference calls have a 3\\,s per-estimate deadline.\n244:\\paragraph{Contrast variants.} The label-free basis variant uses a continuous piecewise-linear basis of $\\Yhat$ with knots at pooled quantiles, the model size $p\\in\\{1,2,3,5,8\\}$ is chosen by leave-one-out PRESS with a 2\\% improvement margin, the standard error uses leverage-corrected leave-one-out residuals, and a guard keeps the width ratio against PPI++ above 0.85. The stacked variant fits seven calibrators (isotonic, three binned with 3, 5 and 8 bins, monotone and local-linear kernels, linear), combines them by non-negative least squares with 4 inner folds, repeats $K=5$ cross-fitting $R=5$ times under a time budget, and uses averaged-residual variance plus a split-dispersion term with $n-3$ degrees of freedom.\n277:Search pools: galaxy morphology (Galaxy Zoo-type crowd labels, \\citealt{Willett2013GalaxyZ2}; $n=300$, $N=5000$), Amazon deforestation ($n=100$, $N=1000$), gene expression ($n=500$, $N=10000$), protein disorder ($n=300$, $N=5000$), and synthetic regimes, 60 draws per family, reset seeds 1001--1005. The sealed evaluation used 680 episodes (500 main and 180 control) and took 1400 seconds of the 5400-second budget. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n283:\\caption{Pool-level moments used for the ceiling (isotonic estimate of $\\eta^2$; synthetic rows are large-sample regime moments). $\\lambda^\\star$ is the unclipped optimal weight on $\\Yhat$ at $n=500$, $N=20000$, not applicable (---) to the synthetic regimes, which mix several $(n,N)$ cells.}\n293:plankton (binary $\\Yhat$) & 0.7004 & 0.7004 & 0.0000 & 0.841 \\\\","stderr